WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
Every run of a pipeline
needs two different pages.

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:06.100
Hi, I'm Tanmay Kulkarni,
for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.240
This week I contributed to Mycroft,

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.880
a public repository of recipes that an
agent can run and a person can read.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.680
Picture an AI pipeline that
has just finished a run.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.620
Somebody needs to know what happened.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.960
But who, exactly?

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.520
There are really two readers.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.960
One is a program,
the next script in the line.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.240
The other is a person, who has
to decide whether to carry on.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.500
They need different pages.

00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:33.053
In the Mycroft repository I built both.

00:00:33.353 --> 00:00:34.773
The machine's page is a log.

00:00:34.973 --> 00:00:36.753
16 fields, built for a script.

00:00:37.113 --> 00:00:39.073
One of them says records seen: 12.

00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:41.973
The person's page is a report.

00:00:42.193 --> 00:00:44.553
It names its reader
and the decision it lets them make,

00:00:44.653 --> 00:00:48.893
and in its first section, it says:
12 requests read. Same fact. Same run.

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:52.553
They agree because both
come from one set of facts,

00:00:52.573 --> 00:00:55.533
and the counts are compared
before the report is written.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:59.167
Then the report adds
the part only a person needs.

00:00:59.487 --> 00:01:02.027
Approve the next phase,
sample mode only: yes.

00:01:02.347 --> 00:01:03.667
Block live execution: yes.

00:01:03.687 --> 00:01:06.087
Computed from the run's files, not typed.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.687
Reading it as a person
is how I found a mistake:

00:01:09.987 --> 00:01:13.247
the log said a stop condition had
triggered, and the report said nothing.

00:01:13.727 --> 00:01:15.827
Now it says: 0 of 4 triggered.

00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:20.007
So for your own pipeline,
ask who reads its last output,

00:01:20.127 --> 00:01:22.527
what they do next,
and what must be on their page.

00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:26.667
That is the idea behind
the long film: Two Customers,

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:30.367
One Run: Writing a Pipeline's
Results for a Program and a Person.

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:35.187
Tanmay Kulkarni,
for Humanitarians AI, signing off.
